WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.488 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.488 --> 00:00:03.472 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.472 --> 00:00:05.952 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.952 --> 00:00:19.870 align:middle line:90%


00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.090 align:middle line:84%
CASEY RODRIGUEZ: All
right, so let's continue

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.070 align:middle line:84%
with our discussion of
measurable functions.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:39.820 align:middle line:84%
So last time we introduced the
notion of measurable functions.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:59.170 align:middle line:84%
So if I have a
measurable set, E from--

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:01.600 align:middle line:84%
or f from E to the
extended real numbers

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.410 align:middle line:84%
is measurable, or is
a measurable function,

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:20.490 align:middle line:84%
if for all alpha in R, the
inverse image of the half

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:25.200 align:middle line:84%
infinite open interval alpha
to infinity is measurable.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:33.670 align:middle line:90%


00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.120 align:middle line:84%
And then we proved that if
we have a function which

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.230 align:middle line:84%
is measurable,
then not only does

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.690 align:middle line:84%
is the pre-image of these
intervals measurable,

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:49.090 align:middle line:84%
but the pre-image of any Borel
set, any member of the Borel

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.460 align:middle line:84%
sigma algebra, which includes
open sets, close sets,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.940 align:middle line:84%
and so on, the pre-image of
those sets is also measurable.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:57.720 align:middle line:90%


00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.960 align:middle line:84%
And we proved that
being measurable

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.300 align:middle line:84%
is closed undertaking sups,
imps, lim sups, lim imps,

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.440 align:middle line:84%
limits in particular,
and changing

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.540 align:middle line:90%
the function a little bit on--

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.030 align:middle line:84%
or changing a function on
the set of measure 0 also

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.580 align:middle line:90%
preserves measurability.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.960 align:middle line:84%
And also being
measurable is closed

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.700 align:middle line:84%
under the algebraic operations
of taking linear combinations

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.450 align:middle line:90%
and products.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.030 align:middle line:90%


00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.670 align:middle line:84%
Now, this definition and
properties we've worked out

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:38.110 align:middle line:84%
was for extended
real-valued functions.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.720 align:middle line:84%
Quite often we'll
be dealing with--

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.540 align:middle line:84%
or in general,
functions that take

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.020 align:middle line:90%
values in the complex numbers.

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:59.432 align:middle line:84%
So let me define what it means
for a complex value function

00:02:59.432 --> 00:03:00.140 align:middle line:90%
to be measurable.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.300 align:middle line:90%
It's not too crazy.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.580 align:middle line:84%
Let E be a subset
of R be measurable.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:15.440 align:middle line:84%
We say a function f from E
now to the complex numbers

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.420 align:middle line:84%
is measurable if the
two functions given

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:26.120 align:middle line:84%
by the real part of f,
which now goes from E to R

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.480 align:middle line:84%
and the imaginary part of f,
which now is a function from E

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:42.220 align:middle line:90%
to R, are measurable.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.930 align:middle line:90%


00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.840 align:middle line:84%
So function into
the complex numbers

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:56.630 align:middle line:84%
you can always write as f
equals the real part of f

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:00.410 align:middle line:84%
plus the imaginary
part of f times i.

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:04.070 align:middle line:84%
So we just-- to say
that f is measurable,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:09.360 align:middle line:84%
just require that it's real and
imaginary parts are measurable,

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:09.860 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:16.640 align:middle line:90%


00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.700 align:middle line:84%
And then you can verify the
following simple theorem.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.570 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll put it on assignment
or not, haven't decided yet--

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:27.050 align:middle line:90%
or just parts of it.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:36.570 align:middle line:84%
If f G were measurable--
and I didn't say

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.360 align:middle line:84%
so, but if we're talking
about measurable functions ,

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.270 align:middle line:84%
the domain E, the domain
is always assumed to be

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.670 align:middle line:90%
a measurable subset of R.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:54.070 align:middle line:84%
So if these are measurable
functions and alpha is

00:04:54.070 --> 00:05:00.810 align:middle line:84%
a complex number,
then the function's

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:07.218 align:middle line:84%
alpha times f, f
plus g, f times g--

00:05:07.218 --> 00:05:08.760 align:middle line:84%
and then we can do
a few other things

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.900 align:middle line:84%
to complex numbers
that change it.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:22.440 align:middle line:84%
The complex conjugate
of f and modulus of f

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:27.360 align:middle line:90%
are measurable functions.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:37.080 align:middle line:90%


00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.635 align:middle line:84%
And then we have the
following theorem.

00:05:40.635 --> 00:05:45.400 align:middle line:90%


00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:55.950 align:middle line:84%
If fn from E to C is
measurable for all n.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:06:21.310 align:middle line:84%
And these functions converge
pointwise to a function f,

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:27.320 align:middle line:84%
then the limiting function
is measurable, OK.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.990 align:middle line:90%


00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.400 align:middle line:90%
And this follows, again--

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.550 align:middle line:84%
so both of these theorems
follow immediately

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:39.450 align:middle line:84%
from what we know
about measurable

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:43.330 align:middle line:90%
extended real-value functions.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:52.000 align:middle line:84%
So, for example, this one
here follows from the fact

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.720 align:middle line:84%
that limit n goes
to infinity of fn

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:09.930 align:middle line:84%
of x equals f of x if
and only if limit as n

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:14.240 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of the
real part of f of f,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:23.830 align:middle line:84%
fn of x equals the real part
of f of x, and the limit as n

00:07:23.830 --> 00:07:26.620 align:middle line:84%
goes to infinity of
the imaginary part

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:33.850 align:middle line:84%
fn of f of x equals the
imaginary part of f of x.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.790 align:middle line:84%
And if we're assuming fn
is measurable for all n,

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:40.150 align:middle line:84%
then the real part and
imaginary parts of fn

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:42.580 align:middle line:90%
are measurable for all n.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.530 align:middle line:84%
And therefore there
are pointwise limits,

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.290 align:middle line:84%
which is the real part of
f and imaginary part of f

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:50.980 align:middle line:84%
are measurable by
the theorem we proved

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:56.480 align:middle line:84%
about extended real-value
measurable functions.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:07:58.960 align:middle line:84%
And so we conclude
that the real part

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.120 align:middle line:84%
of f and the imaginary
part are measurable,

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:05.270 align:middle line:90%
and therefore f is measurable.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:07.980 align:middle line:84%
So you don't have
to work very hard.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:10.940 align:middle line:84%
You can just use
of what we proved

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:15.470 align:middle line:84%
from the previous lecture about
extended real-valued functions

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:16.744 align:middle line:90%
which are measurable.

00:08:16.744 --> 00:08:32.070 align:middle line:90%


00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:38.820 align:middle line:84%
All right, so now as far
as measurable functions go,

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:47.100 align:middle line:84%
we've shown that if I have a
continuous function, then that

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.990 align:middle line:84%
is-- so this is from
the previous time,

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.660 align:middle line:84%
from previous lecture,
continuous functions

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:52.350 align:middle line:90%
are measurable.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:58.530 align:middle line:84%
And if I have a
measurable subset of E,

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:01.930 align:middle line:84%
then the indicator function
of that set is measurable.

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.305 align:middle line:84%
And we know that
linear combinations

00:09:04.305 --> 00:09:05.930 align:middle line:84%
of measurable functions
are measurable.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:11.070 align:middle line:84%
So if I take linear combinations
of indicator functions

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:15.060 align:middle line:84%
with, say, complex
coefficients, then that

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:18.150 align:middle line:90%
will remain measurable as well.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:22.440 align:middle line:84%
And those functions are the
simplest type in that they only

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.900 align:middle line:90%
take finitely many values.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.790 align:middle line:84%
They're so simple
that we glorify them

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:31.035 align:middle line:90%
by giving them that name.

00:09:31.035 --> 00:09:33.890 align:middle line:90%


00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:38.760 align:middle line:84%
And we'll show that
every measurable function

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:42.430 align:middle line:84%
is, in a sense, approximately
a simple function.

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:54.780 align:middle line:84%
So we have the following
definition, if E is measurable,

00:09:54.780 --> 00:10:06.940 align:middle line:84%
a measurable function phi
from E to C is simple,

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:10.090 align:middle line:90%
or we call it a simple function.

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:17.050 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say is
a simple function

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:30.295 align:middle line:84%
if the range of E phi of E is
equal to finitely many values.

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:36.650 align:middle line:90%


00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:39.950 align:middle line:84%
So a measurable function
is a simple function

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.980 align:middle line:90%
if its range is finite.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:48.130 align:middle line:84%
So let me make a general
remark about simple functions.

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:51.430 align:middle line:84%
And also when I write that phi
of E is equal to a1 up to a n,

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:54.230 align:middle line:90%
this is a set.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:57.190 align:middle line:84%
And when I write
a1, a2, up to a n,

00:10:57.190 --> 00:10:59.650 align:middle line:90%
I am implicitly writing here.

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:01.000 align:middle line:90%
I'm not saying the set--

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:02.680 align:middle line:84%
I'm saying each
of these elements

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.870 align:middle line:90%
are different from each other.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:08.050 align:middle line:84%
So a simple function that
just takes the value 1,

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:11.930 align:middle line:84%
I would not write its
range as 1, 1, 1, 1.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:17.050 align:middle line:84%
So although that's a kind
of simple and silly remark

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:19.240 align:middle line:84%
to make, I just
want to make it now.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:24.690 align:middle line:90%


00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:33.530 align:middle line:84%
So if phi is a simple
function, then we

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:42.860 align:middle line:84%
can write it in a
canonical way with--

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.830 align:middle line:84%
so if phi is a simple
function with phi of E

00:11:45.830 --> 00:12:03.860 align:middle line:84%
equals a1 up to a n, then
for all i, the set a sub i,

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:10.040 align:middle line:84%
which is equal to the
inverse image of--

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:15.960 align:middle line:84%
so here phi is
going from E to C.

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:23.490 align:middle line:84%
The inverse image of the
single set, singleton,

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:28.210 align:middle line:90%
which is a closed--

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.315 align:middle line:90%
well, this is going to be--

00:12:31.315 --> 00:12:35.010 align:middle line:90%


00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:39.900 align:middle line:84%
so the inverse image
of this guy here

00:12:39.900 --> 00:12:50.830 align:middle line:84%
is measurable because it's
equal to the intersection of two

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:51.790 align:middle line:90%
measurable sets.

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:56.860 align:middle line:84%
It's equal to the intersection
of when the real part of phi

00:12:56.860 --> 00:13:01.900 align:middle line:84%
equals the real part of a sub
i intersect the set of all x's

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.880 align:middle line:84%
where the imaginary
part of phi equals

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.860 align:middle line:90%
the imaginary part of a sub i.

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:14.340 align:middle line:84%
So that's why the set is
measurable, is measurable.

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:19.185 align:middle line:84%
And we have a few
properties of this guy.

00:13:19.185 --> 00:13:24.450 align:middle line:90%


00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:28.540 align:middle line:84%
If I take two different
elements in the range--

00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:30.450 align:middle line:84%
again, this is why
I made that comment

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:33.060 align:middle line:84%
that when I write
the range this way,

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:37.530 align:middle line:84%
I'm listing the distinct
elements of the range--

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.000 align:middle line:90%
or the image of E--

00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:46.720 align:middle line:84%
For all i equals not equal to
j, these two sets are disjoint.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.170 align:middle line:90%


00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:55.970 align:middle line:84%
And if I take the union
of i equals 1 to n

00:13:55.970 --> 00:14:00.570 align:middle line:84%
of the ai's, this is
equal to the total set E

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:02.850 align:middle line:84%
because this is just
the inverse image

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:09.500 align:middle line:84%
of this set, which is just E,
since E of E equals that, OK.

00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:12.280 align:middle line:90%


00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:23.060 align:middle line:84%
And finally for
all x in E, I can

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:29.700 align:middle line:84%
write phi of x as sum
from i equals 1 to n

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:37.180 align:middle line:90%
of ai chi of a sub i of x.

00:14:37.180 --> 00:14:41.290 align:middle line:84%
So for a simple function, I
can write it in a canonical way

00:14:41.290 --> 00:14:46.270 align:middle line:84%
where it's just a linear
combination of indicator

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:49.240 align:middle line:84%
functions where the sets
those indicator functions

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:53.020 align:middle line:84%
are non-zero on or
disjoint from each other.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:15:00.245 align:middle line:84%
And their union gives
me E, the domain, OK.

00:15:00.245 --> 00:15:08.030 align:middle line:90%


00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:13.702 align:middle line:84%
So these three are the simple
but important properties

00:15:13.702 --> 00:15:15.285 align:middle line:84%
of how to represent
a simple function.

00:15:15.285 --> 00:15:19.450 align:middle line:90%


00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:22.750 align:middle line:84%
So it's not difficult
to verify, again,

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:25.710 align:middle line:84%
just from the definition
of a simple function,

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:53.920 align:middle line:84%
that scalar multiples, linear
combinations, and products

00:15:53.920 --> 00:16:05.130 align:middle line:84%
of simple functions are,
again, simple functions.

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:23.240 align:middle line:90%


00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:26.240 align:middle line:84%
So I've said that
these functions are

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:29.420 align:middle line:84%
so simple that that's
what we call them,

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:36.980 align:middle line:84%
and that they are somehow
universal that, in a sense,

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:40.445 align:middle line:84%
any measurable function is
almost a simple function.

00:16:40.445 --> 00:16:44.340 align:middle line:90%


00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.180 align:middle line:90%
So in what sense do I mean that?

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:50.920 align:middle line:84%
So that's the content of
the following theorem,

00:16:50.920 --> 00:16:59.140 align:middle line:90%
which is that if f from E to--

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:02.770 align:middle line:84%
and let's first--
we're going to do

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:05.410 align:middle line:84%
this for extended
real-valued functions.

00:17:05.410 --> 00:17:08.859 align:middle line:84%
And then the proof will
essentially carry over

00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:09.980 align:middle line:90%
to the complex value.

00:17:09.980 --> 00:17:13.390 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to do it
for the extended real-value

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:16.869 align:middle line:90%
non-negative functions.

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:20.140 align:middle line:84%
And, again, I'll indicate
what the difference

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:23.690 align:middle line:84%
is once we go to complex
valued measurable functions.

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:29.070 align:middle line:90%
So if E is from--

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:32.870 align:middle line:84%
so now this is an extended real
value, but it's non-negative.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:37.710 align:middle line:84%
So if this is a
measurable function,

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:52.290 align:middle line:84%
then there exists a
sequence of simple functions

00:17:52.290 --> 00:18:00.630 align:middle line:84%
phi sub n such that
three things hold.

00:18:00.630 --> 00:18:02.670 align:middle line:90%
We can do this--

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.100 align:middle line:84%
let's write down--
let's go over here.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:13.860 align:middle line:90%


00:18:13.860 --> 00:18:16.180 align:middle line:90%
three things hold.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.135 align:middle line:90%
For all x in E--

00:18:18.135 --> 00:18:23.670 align:middle line:90%


00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:28.140 align:middle line:84%
or let me hold off
on stating that.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:30.330 align:middle line:84%
F dominates these
simple functions.

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:34.200 align:middle line:84%
And these simple functions
are pointwise increasing.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:39.336 align:middle line:84%
For all x in E, 0 is less
than or equal to phi 1 of--

00:18:39.336 --> 00:18:43.480 align:middle line:84%
phi 0 of x is less than or
equal to phi 1 of x is less than

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:48.860 align:middle line:84%
or equal to phi
2 of x and so on.

00:18:48.860 --> 00:18:54.380 align:middle line:90%
And they all sit below f of x.

00:18:54.380 --> 00:19:08.480 align:middle line:84%
For all x in E, these phi's
are converging to f of x.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:12.170 align:middle line:84%
So A and B says
that there exists

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:15.080 align:middle line:84%
a sequence, a measure
of simple functions

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.290 align:middle line:90%
that increase to f of x.

00:19:18.290 --> 00:19:20.735 align:middle line:84%
The last part is that
if this function,

00:19:20.735 --> 00:19:24.470 align:middle line:84%
if this measurable
function is bounded,

00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:27.440 align:middle line:84%
or wherever it's bounded,
this convergence is not just

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:28.475 align:middle line:90%
pointwise but uniform.

00:19:28.475 --> 00:19:31.360 align:middle line:90%


00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:35.380 align:middle line:84%
For all B bigger
than or equal to 0,

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:44.500 align:middle line:84%
the sequence phi n converges
to f uniformly on the set

00:19:44.500 --> 00:19:45.685 align:middle line:90%
where f is bounded.

00:19:45.685 --> 00:19:56.150 align:middle line:90%


00:19:56.150 --> 00:20:01.490 align:middle line:84%
So, again, the take home is that
for every non-negative extended

00:20:01.490 --> 00:20:03.650 align:middle line:84%
real-value measurable
function, we

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:06.050 align:middle line:84%
can find a sequence
of simple functions

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:09.470 align:middle line:90%
that well approximates f.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:12.230 align:middle line:84%
And in what sense does
this will approximate f,

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:13.970 align:middle line:90%
they increase to f.

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:18.440 align:middle line:84%
And if f is bounded, then
that convergence is, in fact,

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:19.070 align:middle line:90%
uniform.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.910 align:middle line:84%
Or more precisely,
wherever f is bounded,

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:25.980 align:middle line:90%
convergence is uniform.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:30.860 align:middle line:84%
So this is in what sense
every measurable function is

00:20:30.860 --> 00:20:32.630 align:middle line:90%
almost a simple function.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:47.850 align:middle line:84%
So let me-- let's get
started with the proof.

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:50.660 align:middle line:84%
And first I'm going to
draw a few pictures so

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:54.905 align:middle line:84%
that you can get the idea of
how we're going to do this,

00:20:54.905 --> 00:20:56.780 align:middle line:84%
or how we're going to
construct this sequence

00:20:56.780 --> 00:21:05.990 align:middle line:84%
of simple functions, and then
turn that into math which might

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:09.290 align:middle line:84%
be a little bit jarring if I
went that route first and then

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:12.073 align:middle line:90%
drew pictures.

00:21:12.073 --> 00:21:13.490 align:middle line:84%
And this picture
I'm going to draw

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:18.440 align:middle line:84%
kind of looks like what I was
talking about where we split

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:22.430 align:middle line:84%
the range up rather than the
domain when we were motivating

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:25.250 align:middle line:84%
why we would even
introduce the concept

00:21:25.250 --> 00:21:26.670 align:middle line:90%
of a measurable function.

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:32.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's say we
have our function f.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:36.600 align:middle line:90%


00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:41.820 align:middle line:84%
Now how I'm going to build
these phi's are that--

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.320 align:middle line:90%
what I'm going to do is--

00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:47.310 align:middle line:84%
so here I'm going
to draw phi 0 now.

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:48.370 align:middle line:90%
Where's the yellow chalk.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:53.440 align:middle line:90%


00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:58.300 align:middle line:84%
What I do is this will
indicate the power of 2,

00:21:58.300 --> 00:22:02.770 align:middle line:84%
how high up I'm going, and
also the resolution, how

00:22:02.770 --> 00:22:08.120 align:middle line:84%
much of my dividing how high up
I'm going into smaller parts.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:12.670 align:middle line:84%
So phi 0 you should think
that my height will be-- well,

00:22:12.670 --> 00:22:17.090 align:middle line:90%
my height is going to be 1.

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:24.550 align:middle line:84%
And so what I do is, how I
define this simple function

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:31.870 align:middle line:84%
phi 0 is I look at where f
is above the final value 1.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:36.490 align:middle line:84%
And my simple function will
be that final value 1 there.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:43.910 align:middle line:90%


00:22:43.910 --> 00:22:47.530 align:middle line:90%
And move this over a little bit.

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:52.640 align:middle line:90%


00:22:52.640 --> 00:23:01.210 align:middle line:84%
And that just leaves
where f is less than 1.

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:10.170 align:middle line:84%
And there I set the value of
my simple function to be 0,

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:15.090 align:middle line:84%
and 0 simply because
that's the lower bound,

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:20.790 align:middle line:84%
or that's the smallest
value of this interval 0, 1.

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:24.060 align:middle line:90%
And that's how I define phi 0.

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:25.680 align:middle line:90%
It goes up to height 1.

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:33.600 align:middle line:84%
And I only split
each part into 1.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.250 align:middle line:90%
So there's only one part here.

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:38.520 align:middle line:84%
Now maybe some of that
didn't make any sense to you.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:39.570 align:middle line:90%
That's OK.

00:23:39.570 --> 00:23:41.070 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
phi 1 and then I'm

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:45.780 align:middle line:84%
going to stop there because this
is going to grow exponentially,

00:23:45.780 --> 00:23:49.500 align:middle line:84%
which means I'll probably
draw an exponentially

00:23:49.500 --> 00:23:53.080 align:middle line:90%
worse picture each time.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:55.500 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going
to draw phi 2.

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:57.240 align:middle line:90%
And what I do is--

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:02.040 align:middle line:90%
there's one-- or phi 1, sorry.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:05.100 align:middle line:84%
One, again, should
indicate the power of 2

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:09.670 align:middle line:84%
that I'm both going up
and resolving the axis.

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:13.140 align:middle line:90%
So now I go up to--

00:24:13.140 --> 00:24:16.570 align:middle line:90%
let's make this a--

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:20.220 align:middle line:84%
so, again, this will
not be quite the scale,

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:24.157 align:middle line:90%
but hopefully that's OK.

00:24:24.157 --> 00:24:27.900 align:middle line:90%


00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:30.045 align:middle line:84%
So let's make this a
little closer to scale.

00:24:30.045 --> 00:24:34.150 align:middle line:90%


00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:39.790 align:middle line:84%
OK, so one, now I have
two parts, 2 to the 1.

00:24:39.790 --> 00:24:45.330 align:middle line:90%


00:24:45.330 --> 00:24:49.920 align:middle line:90%
So this is parameterized how--

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:56.150 align:middle line:84%
tallest I'm going and cutting
up the range of my function f.

00:24:56.150 --> 00:25:05.210 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm going to now
resolve each part in 1/2, 2

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:06.380 align:middle line:90%
to the minus 1.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:07.700 align:middle line:90%
So now I take--

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:10.950 align:middle line:90%


00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:18.840 align:middle line:84%
this is now 3/2, and this is
now 1/2, 2 to the minus 1.

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.480 align:middle line:84%
So why am I writing
to 2 to the minus 1?

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:26.980 align:middle line:84%
Again because this one
here corresponds to--

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.840 align:middle line:84%
and that one here
corresponds to--

00:25:28.840 --> 00:25:33.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm cutting the whole
increments into halves.

00:25:33.700 --> 00:25:38.230 align:middle line:84%
If I go on to phi 2, I'm
going to be going up to 2

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:43.780 align:middle line:84%
to the 2, which is now 4; and
from 4 to 3; 3 to 2; 2 to 1;

00:25:43.780 --> 00:25:47.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to be cutting
those into fourths.

00:25:47.860 --> 00:25:51.490 align:middle line:84%
And, again, what I
do is I look at--

00:25:51.490 --> 00:26:10.660 align:middle line:84%
I cut-- and now I look
at where the function is

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:11.710 align:middle line:90%
in these widths.

00:26:11.710 --> 00:26:14.320 align:middle line:84%
So if it's above
my highest bound,

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:20.500 align:middle line:84%
then my simple function will
be 2 on that set of x's where

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:25.970 align:middle line:84%
it's at its highest,
or where f goes

00:26:25.970 --> 00:26:30.380 align:middle line:84%
past the largest number which
I'm resolving the axis in.

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:33.400 align:middle line:90%


00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:35.365 align:middle line:90%
And then now here, for example--

00:26:35.365 --> 00:26:42.700 align:middle line:90%


00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:45.420 align:middle line:84%
so you should try and draw
your own picture on not just

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:48.600 align:middle line:84%
go off mine because, again,
mine is looking kind of rough

00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:49.230 align:middle line:90%
already.

00:26:49.230 --> 00:26:52.980 align:middle line:84%
But now I look at the
function-- the set

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:57.060 align:middle line:84%
of X's where the function
is between 2 and 3/2.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:01.320 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to be
this piece and this piece.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.210 align:middle line:84%
And then I set my
simple function

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:09.240 align:middle line:84%
to be equal to the value, the
lower bound on this interval

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:10.560 align:middle line:90%
that I've cut the range into.

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:15.010 align:middle line:90%


00:27:15.010 --> 00:27:17.230 align:middle line:84%
And then I do that
from 3/2 to 1.

00:27:17.230 --> 00:27:20.440 align:middle line:84%
And, for example, that's
happening here on this x.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:21.850 align:middle line:90%
And I set it equal to--

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:25.450 align:middle line:84%
I set my simple function equal
to, again, the lower bound

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:29.890 align:middle line:84%
on this interval that I've cut
the range up into, which is 1,

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:34.780 align:middle line:90%
and so, and so on.

00:27:34.780 --> 00:27:40.030 align:middle line:90%


00:27:40.030 --> 00:27:43.048 align:middle line:84%
And now the function
between 1 and 1/2, so this

00:27:43.048 --> 00:27:44.340 align:middle line:90%
is kind of only the last piece.

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:45.930 align:middle line:90%
Everywhere else is filled in.

00:27:45.930 --> 00:27:48.550 align:middle line:84%
And there's no f
in between here,

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:52.140 align:middle line:90%
so I don't assign a value there.

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:55.970 align:middle line:90%


00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:58.540 align:middle line:84%
All right, now that
was me talking my way

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:01.405 align:middle line:84%
through how this function how
the sequence of functions look.

00:28:01.405 --> 00:28:04.000 align:middle line:90%


00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:08.560 align:middle line:84%
Now I'll just write down how
these functions are defined.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:11.400 align:middle line:90%


00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:29.500 align:middle line:84%
So for n equals 0, 1, 2,
and so on for k between 2

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:44.600 align:middle line:84%
to the 2n minus 1,
I define sets E kn.

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:49.850 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the
set of all X's in E such

00:28:49.850 --> 00:29:04.640 align:middle line:84%
that f of x is between
k times 2 to the minus n

00:29:04.640 --> 00:29:06.650 align:middle line:84%
is less than or
equal to k plus 1 2

00:29:06.650 --> 00:29:18.300 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n, which is just
me explicitly writing out

00:29:18.300 --> 00:29:21.510 align:middle line:84%
that this is the
inverse image of k times

00:29:21.510 --> 00:29:28.280 align:middle line:84%
2 to the minus n k plus 1
2 to the minus n closed.

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:30.270 align:middle line:90%
And this is an interval.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:33.200 align:middle line:84%
And since f is measurable,
assumed to be measurable,

00:29:33.200 --> 00:29:36.290 align:middle line:84%
this is a measurable-- the
inverse image of that interval

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:41.110 align:middle line:90%
is a measurable set, OK.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:55.170 align:middle line:84%
And then I define fn to be the
inverse image of when f exceeds

00:29:55.170 --> 00:30:02.250 align:middle line:84%
my top value of how I'm cutting
up the range, 2 the n, again,

00:30:02.250 --> 00:30:03.270 align:middle line:90%
which is measurable.

00:30:03.270 --> 00:30:06.530 align:middle line:90%


00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:11.510 align:middle line:84%
And, finally, I will take
my simple function phi n

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:22.010 align:middle line:84%
to be sum from k equals 0 to
2 to the 2n minus 1, k times 2

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:24.540 align:middle line:90%
to the minus n, which is--

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:26.030 align:middle line:90%
again, this is the lower--

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:30.290 align:middle line:84%
this would correspond to
the lower part of my E kn

00:30:30.290 --> 00:30:31.170 align:middle line:90%
that I'm looking at.

00:30:31.170 --> 00:30:36.110 align:middle line:84%
So for this example, if
this Ekn is the two 3/2,

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:57.810 align:middle line:84%
and 3/2 is the lower part times
chi E kn plus 2 to the n times

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:01.560 align:middle line:90%
where f exceeds 2 to the n.

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:08.970 align:middle line:84%
So I encourage you
to maybe write out

00:31:08.970 --> 00:31:12.360 align:middle line:90%
phi 1, what that actually is.

00:31:12.360 --> 00:31:17.130 align:middle line:84%
I mean, there's-- in
fact here, I'll do that.

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:20.070 align:middle line:90%


00:31:20.070 --> 00:31:22.800 align:middle line:84%
I drew the picture
that goes over here.

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:25.110 align:middle line:84%
Let me write out what phi
1 actually looks like.

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:29.550 align:middle line:84%
Phi 1 is equal to 0
times the indicator

00:31:29.550 --> 00:31:37.620 align:middle line:84%
function where f is
between 0 and 1/2

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:42.060 align:middle line:84%
plus a 1/2 times the
indicator function where

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:54.040 align:middle line:84%
f is between 1/2 and 1 plus
3/2 times the indicator

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:58.210 align:middle line:90%
function of when f is between--

00:31:58.210 --> 00:31:59.248 align:middle line:90%
no, that's not 3/2.

00:31:59.248 --> 00:32:00.040 align:middle line:90%
That should be 1/2.

00:32:00.040 --> 00:32:03.780 align:middle line:90%


00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.460 align:middle line:90%
No, no, no, what am I doing, 1--

00:32:05.460 --> 00:32:08.550 align:middle line:90%


00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:13.980 align:middle line:84%
times indicator function
where f is between 1 and 3/2

00:32:13.980 --> 00:32:20.340 align:middle line:84%
plus 3/2 times chi
f inverse of where

00:32:20.340 --> 00:32:26.340 align:middle line:90%
f is between now 3/2 and 2.

00:32:26.340 --> 00:32:29.040 align:middle line:90%


00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:33.180 align:middle line:84%
So 2 is how high up
I break up the range.

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:36.040 align:middle line:84%
And then plus this last
part, this fn part,

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:40.260 align:middle line:90%
which is 2 times chi, where f--

00:32:40.260 --> 00:32:47.020 align:middle line:84%
the indicator function of where
f is bigger than 2 to the n,

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:48.940 align:middle line:90%
So bigger than 2.

00:32:48.940 --> 00:33:01.430 align:middle line:90%


00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:07.130 align:middle line:84%
So all of these
sets are disjoint.

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:10.580 align:middle line:84%
The Ekn's and fn's
for k different from k

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:14.750 align:middle line:90%
prime and fixed n are disjoint.

00:33:14.750 --> 00:33:16.500 align:middle line:84%
And they are disjoint
from this set.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:20.270 align:middle line:84%
So this is a simple function,
the finitely many values

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:23.870 align:middle line:84%
it takes is 2 to the n along
with k times 2 to the minus n.

00:33:23.870 --> 00:33:25.700 align:middle line:84%
Or at least the
finitely many values

00:33:25.700 --> 00:33:27.840 align:middle line:90%
it can take is a subset of that.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:30.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a simple
function for each n.

00:33:30.980 --> 00:33:34.340 align:middle line:90%


00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:41.080 align:middle line:84%
And by design it's
always sitting below f.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:45.680 align:middle line:84%
So let me in fact
bring it up here.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:48.040 align:middle line:84%
Well, let me make the
statement and then I'll say.

00:33:48.040 --> 00:34:01.010 align:middle line:84%
So by definition for all
x in E, En is non-negative

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:03.860 align:middle line:90%
and always sits below f of x.

00:34:03.860 --> 00:34:10.012 align:middle line:90%
So how do we see that?

00:34:10.012 --> 00:34:11.679 align:middle line:84%
You can see it from
the general formula,

00:34:11.679 --> 00:34:13.699 align:middle line:84%
but I'll just indicate
why just for safety.

00:34:13.699 --> 00:34:17.050 align:middle line:84%
One, let's say-- so x has
to be in one of these sets.

00:34:17.050 --> 00:34:21.940 align:middle line:84%
Let's say it's here where
f is between 1/2 and 1.

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:28.800 align:middle line:84%
Then phi-- 1 of x is equal
to 1/2 which is less than f

00:34:28.800 --> 00:34:32.280 align:middle line:84%
of x because f takes on the
value between 1/2 and 1.

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:41.630 align:middle line:90%


00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:44.704 align:middle line:84%
In fact, here, I'll give
a the brief argument here.

00:34:44.704 --> 00:34:47.310 align:middle line:90%


00:34:47.310 --> 00:34:52.800 align:middle line:84%
If x is in Ekn
then by definition

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:57.780 align:middle line:84%
this means that k2 to the
minus n is less than f

00:34:57.780 --> 00:35:05.550 align:middle line:84%
of x is less than or equal to
k plus 1 2 the minus n, which

00:35:05.550 --> 00:35:10.590 align:middle line:84%
implies that phi n of x, which
is by definition k times 2

00:35:10.590 --> 00:35:11.970 align:middle line:90%
to the minus n--

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:15.060 align:middle line:84%
just, again, by how we've
defined the phi sub n's-- where

00:35:15.060 --> 00:35:16.710 align:middle line:90%
did we define the phi sub n's--

00:35:16.710 --> 00:35:22.190 align:middle line:90%
there is less than f of x.

00:35:22.190 --> 00:35:44.720 align:middle line:90%


00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:47.090 align:middle line:90%
So that's for x and Ekn.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:55.160 align:middle line:84%
And if x is in f
sub n, then that

00:35:55.160 --> 00:36:02.480 align:middle line:84%
means f of x is greater than
2 to the n, which is always

00:36:02.480 --> 00:36:05.420 align:middle line:84%
bigger than or equal to
the phi n of x-- well,

00:36:05.420 --> 00:36:09.710 align:middle line:84%
I mean this is actually
equal to phi of x.

00:36:09.710 --> 00:36:13.880 align:middle line:84%
So we always have-- the
phi n's are non-negative.

00:36:13.880 --> 00:36:15.890 align:middle line:84%
And they always
sit below f of x.

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:20.090 align:middle line:84%
So now let's prove that they
are, in fact, increasing,

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:28.320 align:middle line:90%
so part A.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:33.735 align:middle line:84%
So now we're proving part A,
the phi n's increase to f.

00:36:33.735 --> 00:36:39.490 align:middle line:90%


00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:46.450 align:middle line:84%
And, again, for
fixed n, Ekn and fn,

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:52.270 align:middle line:84%
the Ekn's, and fn form
a disjoint union of E.

00:36:52.270 --> 00:36:57.130 align:middle line:84%
I just need to check that
what I want holds on each

00:36:57.130 --> 00:36:58.570 align:middle line:90%
of the Ekn's and fn's.

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:09.750 align:middle line:84%
So suppose x is in
Ekn, then f of x

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:16.140 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to k
plus 1 times 2 to the minus n.

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:22.100 align:middle line:84%
n is bigger than k
2 to the minus n,

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:27.410 align:middle line:84%
and which by a silly trick of
just multiplying and dividing

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:32.870 align:middle line:84%
by 2 tells me that f is between
2 times k times 2 to the minus

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:36.980 align:middle line:84%
n minus 1 is less than
f of x is less than

00:37:36.980 --> 00:37:42.050 align:middle line:84%
or equal to 2k plus 2
times 2 to the minus

00:37:42.050 --> 00:37:49.580 align:middle line:84%
n minus 1, which
implies that x is

00:37:49.580 --> 00:38:00.350 align:middle line:84%
in the union of E2k n plus
1, 2k n plus 1 union 2K

00:38:00.350 --> 00:38:03.590 align:middle line:90%
plus 2 n plus 1.

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:10.130 align:middle line:90%


00:38:10.130 --> 00:38:17.180 align:middle line:84%
If x is in E2k n plus 1,
then I get that phi n of x

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:20.930 align:middle line:84%
is equal to, by
definition, k times

00:38:20.930 --> 00:38:26.360 align:middle line:84%
2 to the minus n, which
equals 2k 2 to the minus

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:32.090 align:middle line:84%
n minus 1, which because
x is in E 2k n plus 1,

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:36.395 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to
phi n plus 1 of x.

00:38:36.395 --> 00:38:41.210 align:middle line:90%


00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:51.300 align:middle line:84%
And if x is in E2k
plus 2 n plus 1--

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:53.750 align:middle line:84%
no, I shouldn't--
this shouldn't be a 2.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.230 align:middle line:90%
This should be a 1, I'm sorry--

00:38:56.230 --> 00:39:01.030 align:middle line:84%
because this goes from 2k up
to 2k plus 1, and then from 2k

00:39:01.030 --> 00:39:02.860 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 up to 2k plus 2.

00:39:02.860 --> 00:39:07.390 align:middle line:84%
So it's either in
E2k or 2k plus 1.

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:20.410 align:middle line:84%
And if E is in a 2k plus 1,
then the n of x is still--

00:39:20.410 --> 00:39:24.880 align:middle line:84%
I mean, x is still
in Ekn, so the n

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:29.170 align:middle line:84%
of x is equal to still k
times 2 to the minus n, which

00:39:29.170 --> 00:39:33.160 align:middle line:84%
is equal to 2k times 2 to
the minus n minus 1, which

00:39:33.160 --> 00:39:40.480 align:middle line:84%
is less than 2k plus 1 times
2 to the minus n minus 1,

00:39:40.480 --> 00:39:47.320 align:middle line:84%
which is by definition, since
x is in E2k plus 1, n plus 1 n

00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:48.670 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 of x.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:40:01.351 align:middle line:90%


00:40:01.351 --> 00:40:11.430 align:middle line:84%
And, similarly, if x is
in Fn, then the n of x

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:17.160 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to phi n plus 1 of x.

00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:27.960 align:middle line:84%
So we verified for all x since
E is equal to this union over k

00:40:27.960 --> 00:40:38.250 align:middle line:84%
equals 0 2 to the 2n
minus 1 Ekn union Fn.

00:40:38.250 --> 00:40:41.450 align:middle line:90%


00:40:41.450 --> 00:40:44.990 align:middle line:84%
This implies that
for all x in E,

00:40:44.990 --> 00:40:52.445 align:middle line:84%
phi n of x is less than or
equal to phi n plus 1 of x.

00:40:52.445 --> 00:41:08.930 align:middle line:90%


00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:15.080 align:middle line:90%
And this proves A.

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:20.630 align:middle line:90%
Now, how to prove B and C--

00:41:20.630 --> 00:41:23.360 align:middle line:84%
these things will follow from
part A in a simple estimate

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:24.500 align:middle line:90%
that we're going to prove.

00:41:24.500 --> 00:41:32.280 align:middle line:90%


00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:37.010 align:middle line:84%
So B and C will
follow immediately

00:41:37.010 --> 00:41:47.370 align:middle line:84%
from the following claim in
part a, which the claim is that

00:41:47.370 --> 00:42:00.280 align:middle line:84%
for all x in set y in E that f
of y is less than or equal to 2

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:00.850 align:middle line:90%
to the n.

00:42:00.850 --> 00:42:04.840 align:middle line:90%


00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:10.090 align:middle line:84%
This part we already know
that f of x minus phi n of x

00:42:10.090 --> 00:42:11.890 align:middle line:90%
is non-negative.

00:42:11.890 --> 00:42:17.330 align:middle line:84%
But, in fact, this is
bounded by 2 to the minus n.

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:25.720 align:middle line:90%


00:42:25.720 --> 00:42:45.490 align:middle line:84%
So then B and C from
A and this claim.

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:50.280 align:middle line:84%
Why does B follow
from this claim?

00:42:50.280 --> 00:43:02.740 align:middle line:90%
Well, wherever x is--

00:43:02.740 --> 00:43:10.230 align:middle line:90%


00:43:10.230 --> 00:43:12.750 align:middle line:84%
I don't want to have to
also explain what happens

00:43:12.750 --> 00:43:14.580 align:middle line:90%
if f is equal to infinity.

00:43:14.580 --> 00:43:19.410 align:middle line:84%
That also follows basically
from the definitions,

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:20.910 align:middle line:84%
not necessarily
from this estimate.

00:43:20.910 --> 00:43:24.510 align:middle line:84%
But the more important
part is when f is finite.

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:29.340 align:middle line:84%
So let's assume f is
just finite for every x.

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:33.180 align:middle line:84%
So then every x
in E is eventually

00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:35.460 align:middle line:90%
in one of these sets.

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:40.020 align:middle line:90%
So there exist let x be fixed.

00:43:40.020 --> 00:43:45.660 align:middle line:84%
Then for n sufficiently large,
x is in one of these sets

00:43:45.660 --> 00:43:49.140 align:middle line:90%
since f of x is finite.

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:50.430 align:middle line:90%
And therefore for all n--

00:43:50.430 --> 00:43:53.100 align:middle line:90%


00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:58.550 align:middle line:84%
for all capital N
sufficiently large,

00:43:58.550 --> 00:44:01.670 align:middle line:84%
this minus phi n of x is
less than or equal to 2

00:44:01.670 --> 00:44:02.750 align:middle line:90%
to the minus n.

00:44:02.750 --> 00:44:06.140 align:middle line:84%
But then f of x
minus phi to the m

00:44:06.140 --> 00:44:10.010 align:middle line:84%
is also less than or equal to
2 to the minus n for every m

00:44:10.010 --> 00:44:12.020 align:middle line:84%
bigger than or equal
to n because the phi

00:44:12.020 --> 00:44:15.080 align:middle line:90%
n's are increasing.

00:44:15.080 --> 00:44:17.840 align:middle line:84%
If phi n is this
close to f, then phi m

00:44:17.840 --> 00:44:21.620 align:middle line:84%
is also that close to f if m
is bigger than or equal to n,

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:25.910 align:middle line:84%
again, because
they're increasing.

00:44:25.910 --> 00:44:30.650 align:middle line:84%
So that proves-- that's
why pointwise convergence

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:32.810 align:middle line:90%
B follows from this estimate.

00:44:32.810 --> 00:44:35.960 align:middle line:84%
As far as part C, that also
follows from this estimate

00:44:35.960 --> 00:44:40.700 align:middle line:84%
since if I have
a fixed B, then I

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:43.580 align:middle line:84%
can choose a natural number
just depending on what

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:46.615 align:middle line:84%
B is so that that's
set depending

00:44:46.615 --> 00:44:48.740 align:middle line:84%
on B and the original
statement I'm pointing at it,

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:50.782 align:middle line:84%
but I don't think you can
see it from the camera.

00:44:50.782 --> 00:44:55.070 align:middle line:84%
That set which depends on B is
contained in one of these sets,

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:59.690 align:middle line:84%
and therefore for all x in the
set where f is bounded by B,

00:44:59.690 --> 00:45:02.383 align:middle line:90%
this holds uniformly in x.

00:45:02.383 --> 00:45:04.550 align:middle line:84%
And that's where the uniform
convergence comes from.

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:07.440 align:middle line:90%


00:45:07.440 --> 00:45:11.360 align:middle line:84%
But the whole point is that this
is the estimate that gives us

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:16.790 align:middle line:90%
B and C once we've proved A.

00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:18.620 align:middle line:90%
So let's prove this claim.

00:45:18.620 --> 00:45:20.390 align:middle line:90%
It's not hard.

00:45:20.390 --> 00:45:38.690 align:middle line:84%
It's basically because we
are cutting up the range

00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:44.240 align:middle line:84%
not only to height 2 to the
n, but with resolution 2

00:45:44.240 --> 00:45:48.875 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n at
each stage, at each n.

00:45:48.875 --> 00:45:51.960 align:middle line:90%


00:45:51.960 --> 00:46:07.020 align:middle line:84%
So to prove the claim we have
that the set of y's in E such

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:11.490 align:middle line:84%
that f of x is less than
or equal to 2 to the n,

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:18.510 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to the union k
equals 0 2 to the 2n minus 1

00:46:18.510 --> 00:46:22.300 align:middle line:90%
of the Ekn's.

00:46:22.300 --> 00:46:23.790 align:middle line:84%
So if I want to
check that bound,

00:46:23.790 --> 00:46:28.920 align:middle line:84%
I just have to check it for
each, if x is in one of these.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:40.140 align:middle line:84%
So suppose x is in
E to the kn, then--

00:46:40.140 --> 00:46:42.210 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it really is just
following from the fact

00:46:42.210 --> 00:46:48.255 align:middle line:84%
that we're cutting up the
range with resolution 2

00:46:48.255 --> 00:46:49.710 align:middle line:90%
to the minus n.

00:46:49.710 --> 00:46:53.250 align:middle line:84%
So I'm just going to draw a
small little picture here.

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:57.240 align:middle line:90%
We have the axis here.

00:46:57.240 --> 00:47:01.890 align:middle line:84%
And here's k plus 1
times 2 to the minus n.

00:47:01.890 --> 00:47:04.500 align:middle line:90%
Here's k times 2 to the minus n.

00:47:04.500 --> 00:47:09.790 align:middle line:84%
If x is in there,
that means there's a--

00:47:09.790 --> 00:47:12.690 align:middle line:90%


00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:16.290 align:middle line:84%
we're looking at the portion
of f that sits between k 2

00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:23.500 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n and k plus 1 2
to the minus n, then k times

00:47:23.500 --> 00:47:27.910 align:middle line:84%
2 to the minus n is less than f
of x is less than or equal to k

00:47:27.910 --> 00:47:33.010 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 2 to the minus n.

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:37.200 align:middle line:84%
And remember the simple
function on this piece

00:47:37.200 --> 00:47:42.230 align:middle line:90%
evaluated in here--

00:47:42.230 --> 00:47:44.950 align:middle line:90%
so here's the x--

00:47:44.950 --> 00:47:50.730 align:middle line:84%
gives the value at
the lower bound.

00:47:50.730 --> 00:47:57.080 align:middle line:90%


00:47:57.080 --> 00:48:04.470 align:middle line:84%
And therefore we get that fn
of x minus phi n of x, this

00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:05.520 align:middle line:90%
is equal to--

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:10.380 align:middle line:84%
not fn, sorry-- f of x
minus k2 to the minus 1.

00:48:10.380 --> 00:48:12.780 align:middle line:90%
Now x, again, is an Ekn.

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:15.370 align:middle line:84%
So f of x is between
these two numbers.

00:48:15.370 --> 00:48:20.580 align:middle line:84%
So this is less than or
equal to k plus 1 times 2

00:48:20.580 --> 00:48:24.720 align:middle line:84%
to the minus n minus
k 2 to the minus n.

00:48:24.720 --> 00:48:26.550 align:middle line:84%
And this equals
2 to the minus n.

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:31.120 align:middle line:90%


00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:35.380 align:middle line:84%
So whenever we have an
x in one of these sets,

00:48:35.380 --> 00:48:39.550 align:middle line:84%
it has to be within
2 to the minus n.

00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:41.710 align:middle line:84%
The simple function
evaluated at that x

00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:44.710 align:middle line:84%
has to be within 2
to the minus n of f.

00:48:44.710 --> 00:48:51.020 align:middle line:84%
This is just by construction,
by how we've cut up the y-axis,

00:48:51.020 --> 00:48:53.110 align:middle line:90%
if you like, the range.

00:48:53.110 --> 00:48:58.610 align:middle line:84%
We're cutting it up with
resolution 2 to the minus n.

00:48:58.610 --> 00:49:02.420 align:middle line:84%
All right, so that proves the
claim which, as I said along

00:49:02.420 --> 00:49:12.050 align:middle line:90%
with part A, proves B and C.

00:49:12.050 --> 00:49:14.480 align:middle line:84%
So that proves that
every measurable function

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:17.480 align:middle line:84%
is almost at least
non-negative extended

00:49:17.480 --> 00:49:20.060 align:middle line:84%
real valued
measurable function is

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:26.610 align:middle line:84%
a limit of a sequence
of simple functions.

00:49:26.610 --> 00:49:31.220 align:middle line:84%
Now, this theorem carries
over without difficulty

00:49:31.220 --> 00:49:33.260 align:middle line:84%
to complex value
functions after I just

00:49:33.260 --> 00:49:37.710 align:middle line:84%
introduce a breakup of
a function in general.

00:49:37.710 --> 00:49:49.680 align:middle line:84%
So if E is a function from
minus infinity to infinity,

00:49:49.680 --> 00:49:55.870 align:middle line:84%
so an extended
real-value function now,

00:49:55.870 --> 00:50:02.350 align:middle line:84%
we define its positive
and negative part f

00:50:02.350 --> 00:50:09.630 align:middle line:84%
plus of x to equal
the max of f of x, 0.

00:50:09.630 --> 00:50:11.940 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
positive part of F f.

00:50:11.940 --> 00:50:20.410 align:middle line:90%


00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:27.062 align:middle line:84%
f minus of x is
equal to the min of--

00:50:27.062 --> 00:50:27.895 align:middle line:90%
the max, I'm sorry--

00:50:27.895 --> 00:50:31.350 align:middle line:90%


00:50:31.350 --> 00:50:34.800 align:middle line:90%
minus f of x, 0.

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:39.720 align:middle line:90%
This is the negative part of f.

00:50:39.720 --> 00:50:48.040 align:middle line:90%


00:50:48.040 --> 00:50:51.100 align:middle line:84%
So what about these
positive and negative parts?

00:50:51.100 --> 00:50:57.960 align:middle line:84%
Then f is equal to f
plus minus F minus.

00:50:57.960 --> 00:50:59.010 align:middle line:90%
You can just check that.

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:02.040 align:middle line:84%
I mean, take any x if f is
positive or non-negative

00:51:02.040 --> 00:51:05.460 align:middle line:90%
on that, then I get f of x out.

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:10.180 align:middle line:84%
If it is negative,
then I will get

00:51:10.180 --> 00:51:13.480 align:middle line:84%
minus f of x, which is the
absolute value times minus

00:51:13.480 --> 00:51:16.840 align:middle line:90%
gives me back f of x.

00:51:16.840 --> 00:51:24.760 align:middle line:84%
And the absolute value of f is
equal to f plus plus f minus.

00:51:24.760 --> 00:51:34.520 align:middle line:90%


00:51:34.520 --> 00:51:37.310 align:middle line:84%
So let me make a
comment that if--

00:51:37.310 --> 00:51:40.970 align:middle line:84%
this is just a definition for
an arbitrary function from E

00:51:40.970 --> 00:51:42.710 align:middle line:90%
to the extended real numbers--

00:51:42.710 --> 00:51:45.800 align:middle line:84%
if f is measurable, then
each of these functions

00:51:45.800 --> 00:51:47.720 align:middle line:84%
is measurable because
this is-- if you

00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:50.780 align:middle line:84%
like the supremum of the
sequence of functions

00:51:50.780 --> 00:51:53.580 align:middle line:84%
given by f and
then 0 afterwards.

00:51:53.580 --> 00:51:56.660 align:middle line:84%
And this is given by the
supremum of functions

00:51:56.660 --> 00:51:59.480 align:middle line:84%
given by minus f
and 0 afterwards.

00:51:59.480 --> 00:52:03.440 align:middle line:84%
So if f is measurable,
it's positive.

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:06.290 align:middle line:84%
And negative parts are
also measurable functions.

00:52:06.290 --> 00:52:08.630 align:middle line:90%
And they're also non-negative.

00:52:08.630 --> 00:52:16.410 align:middle line:84%
So maybe that wasn't clear, or
at least it should be clear.

00:52:16.410 --> 00:52:19.850 align:middle line:84%
This is the max always involving
0, so it's always non-negative.

00:52:19.850 --> 00:52:29.130 align:middle line:90%


00:52:29.130 --> 00:52:32.610 align:middle line:84%
So now the construction
we did a minute ago

00:52:32.610 --> 00:52:39.270 align:middle line:84%
essentially carries over to
the case of complex value

00:52:39.270 --> 00:52:40.660 align:middle line:90%
measurable functions.

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:53.280 align:middle line:84%
So let E be measurable and
f from E to C be measurable.

00:52:53.280 --> 00:52:57.760 align:middle line:90%


00:52:57.760 --> 00:53:09.570 align:middle line:84%
Then there exists sequence
of simple functions

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:15.230 align:middle line:90%
phi n such that--

00:53:15.230 --> 00:53:16.850 align:middle line:84%
analogs of those
three properties

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:28.610 align:middle line:84%
hold, A is for all x in E.
These are increasing in modulus,

00:53:28.610 --> 00:53:29.480 align:middle line:90%
in absolute value.

00:53:29.480 --> 00:53:31.580 align:middle line:90%
0 is bigger than or equal to--

00:53:31.580 --> 00:53:37.750 align:middle line:84%
of course ph 0 of x is less than
or equal to the absolute value

00:53:37.750 --> 00:53:40.330 align:middle line:84%
of phi 1 of x less
than or equal to the,

00:53:40.330 --> 00:53:42.805 align:middle line:84%
let's say absolute value and
modulus, the same thing--

00:53:42.805 --> 00:53:45.490 align:middle line:90%


00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:51.130 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal to the
absolute value of f of x.

00:53:51.130 --> 00:54:04.000 align:middle line:90%


00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:08.485 align:middle line:84%
These phi n's are
converging to f pointwise.

00:54:08.485 --> 00:54:21.870 align:middle line:90%


00:54:21.870 --> 00:54:25.470 align:middle line:84%
And, finally, convergence
is uniform on sets

00:54:25.470 --> 00:54:26.740 align:middle line:90%
where f is bounded.

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:31.365 align:middle line:84%
So for all B bigger
than or equal to 0--

00:54:31.365 --> 00:54:35.952 align:middle line:90%


00:54:35.952 --> 00:54:39.970 align:middle line:90%
What did I say a minute ago.

00:54:39.970 --> 00:54:54.660 align:middle line:84%
Oh, phi n convergence to f
uniformly on the set x in E

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:59.350 align:middle line:84%
such that the
absolute value of f

00:54:59.350 --> 00:55:08.440 align:middle line:84%
of x, or the modulus of f of
x, is less than or equal to B.

00:55:08.440 --> 00:55:14.800 align:middle line:84%
So this theorem
follows immediately

00:55:14.800 --> 00:55:17.020 align:middle line:84%
from the previous theorem
because now what I do

00:55:17.020 --> 00:55:18.040 align:middle line:90%
is I just take f.

00:55:18.040 --> 00:55:20.650 align:middle line:84%
I split it into its real
and imaginary parts.

00:55:20.650 --> 00:55:23.770 align:middle line:84%
And then I split the imaginary--
the real and imaginary parts

00:55:23.770 --> 00:55:25.630 align:middle line:84%
into both positive
and negative parts.

00:55:25.630 --> 00:55:30.640 align:middle line:84%
So I will leave it to you to
actually fill in the details,

00:55:30.640 --> 00:55:40.800 align:middle line:84%
but you apply the
previous theorem

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:46.020 align:middle line:84%
to the real part
of f plus or minus,

00:55:46.020 --> 00:55:50.460 align:middle line:84%
which are now non-negative
measurable functions.

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:54.740 align:middle line:90%


00:55:54.740 --> 00:55:56.570 align:middle line:84%
And the positive
and negative parts

00:55:56.570 --> 00:56:02.290 align:middle line:84%
of the imaginary part of f,
again, which are non-negative

00:56:02.290 --> 00:56:04.120 align:middle line:84%
measurable functions,
and then you

00:56:04.120 --> 00:56:11.430 align:middle line:84%
just take linear combinations
of these simple functions that

00:56:11.430 --> 00:56:12.465 align:middle line:90%
add up to f.

00:56:12.465 --> 00:56:15.590 align:middle line:90%


00:56:15.590 --> 00:56:18.090 align:middle line:84%
You will take the sequence of
simple functions corresponding

00:56:18.090 --> 00:56:21.900 align:middle line:84%
to the real part of f
and subtract the sequence

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:24.570 align:middle line:84%
of simple functions that
you got for the minus

00:56:24.570 --> 00:56:27.960 align:middle line:84%
for the negative part
of f, real part of f.

00:56:27.960 --> 00:56:34.263 align:middle line:84%
And you will take that and
add i times the positive part

00:56:34.263 --> 00:56:36.180 align:middle line:84%
or the sequence of simple
functions converging

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:39.360 align:middle line:84%
to the positive part of
the imaginary part of f

00:56:39.360 --> 00:56:42.600 align:middle line:84%
minus the sequence of functions,
simple functions converging

00:56:42.600 --> 00:56:46.170 align:middle line:84%
to the negative part of
the imaginary part of f.

00:56:46.170 --> 00:56:48.780 align:middle line:84%
So that's what I mean by
apply previous theorem

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:51.780 align:middle line:84%
to the positive
and negative parts

00:56:51.780 --> 00:56:56.150 align:middle line:84%
of the real and
imaginary parts of f.

00:56:56.150 --> 00:56:59.450 align:middle line:84%
So what's the significance
of this theorem?

00:56:59.450 --> 00:57:04.830 align:middle line:90%
Not only-- let me just say if--

00:57:04.830 --> 00:57:07.650 align:middle line:84%
also showing that somehow
measurable functions

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:09.870 align:middle line:84%
are well approximated or
almost simple functions,

00:57:09.870 --> 00:57:13.920 align:middle line:84%
this also gives us a way of
maybe defining the integral

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:16.980 align:middle line:84%
at least of non-negative
functions that way

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:21.630 align:middle line:84%
we don't have to deal
with possible deals

00:57:21.630 --> 00:57:26.550 align:middle line:84%
with subtracting
infinity from infinity,

00:57:26.550 --> 00:57:31.200 align:middle line:84%
but by simply
defining its integral

00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:38.330 align:middle line:84%
to be the limit
of these integrals

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:39.860 align:middle line:90%
of these simple functions.

00:57:39.860 --> 00:57:43.280 align:middle line:84%
And for a simple function,
we would presumably

00:57:43.280 --> 00:57:44.810 align:middle line:90%
know how to define an integral.

00:57:44.810 --> 00:57:47.810 align:middle line:84%
It would just be the numbers
time the measure of the sets

00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:51.080 align:middle line:84%
that appear in the
indicator functions

00:57:51.080 --> 00:57:52.610 align:middle line:90%
for these simple functions.

00:57:52.610 --> 00:57:56.010 align:middle line:90%


00:57:56.010 --> 00:57:58.080 align:middle line:84%
And like I said
last lecture, if you

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:00.930 align:middle line:84%
wanted to define the
Lebesgue integral that way,

00:58:00.930 --> 00:58:03.630 align:middle line:84%
you would run up against,
well, does this number

00:58:03.630 --> 00:58:06.600 align:middle line:84%
depend on the sequence
of simple functions

00:58:06.600 --> 00:58:09.530 align:middle line:90%
you chose to approximate f?

00:58:09.530 --> 00:58:11.280 align:middle line:84%
But we're not going
to define the Lebesgue

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:12.155 align:middle line:90%
integral in that way.

00:58:12.155 --> 00:58:14.910 align:middle line:84%
We're going to define it a
little bit differently, which

00:58:14.910 --> 00:58:16.750 align:middle line:84%
is what we're going
to move on to now,

00:58:16.750 --> 00:58:20.655 align:middle line:84%
which is the Lebesgue integral
of a non-negative function.

00:58:20.655 --> 00:58:23.220 align:middle line:90%


00:58:23.220 --> 00:58:29.190 align:middle line:84%
And then we will define the
Lebesgue integral, or Lebesgue

00:58:29.190 --> 00:58:30.300 align:middle line:90%
integrable functions.

00:58:30.300 --> 00:58:33.030 align:middle line:84%
These will be complex value
functions now for which

00:58:33.030 --> 00:58:35.700 align:middle line:90%
we can define an integral for.

00:58:35.700 --> 00:58:38.130 align:middle line:90%
And that is the full theory of--

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:45.880 align:middle line:84%
and that's the end of the
game for as far as defining

00:58:45.880 --> 00:58:46.670 align:middle line:90%
Lebesgue integral.

00:58:46.670 --> 00:58:50.530 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll prove
some convergence theorems

00:58:50.530 --> 00:58:53.770 align:middle line:84%
along the way, which make
Lebesgue integral stronger

00:58:53.770 --> 00:58:55.080 align:middle line:90%
than Riemann integral.

00:58:55.080 --> 00:59:04.360 align:middle line:84%
So now we're moving on
to the Lebesgue integral

00:59:04.360 --> 00:59:05.860 align:middle line:90%
of a non-negative function.

00:59:05.860 --> 00:59:15.120 align:middle line:90%


00:59:15.120 --> 00:59:18.360 align:middle line:84%
And why start with a
non-negative function?

00:59:18.360 --> 00:59:21.475 align:middle line:84%
Again, because I just pulled
this trick on you a minute ago,

00:59:21.475 --> 00:59:23.100 align:middle line:84%
that if somehow we
know how to do stuff

00:59:23.100 --> 00:59:26.460 align:middle line:84%
for non-negative
measurable functions,

00:59:26.460 --> 00:59:28.860 align:middle line:84%
then by playing
this game where we

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:30.420 align:middle line:84%
take the real and
imaginary parts

00:59:30.420 --> 00:59:33.090 align:middle line:84%
and split it up into
positive and negative parts,

00:59:33.090 --> 00:59:35.740 align:middle line:84%
hopefully we can do something
for general functions.

00:59:35.740 --> 00:59:41.098 align:middle line:84%
So that's why we start out
with introducing or defining

00:59:41.098 --> 00:59:43.140 align:middle line:84%
the Lebesgue integral of
a non-negative function.

00:59:43.140 --> 00:59:45.700 align:middle line:90%


00:59:45.700 --> 00:59:56.940 align:middle line:84%
So definition, if E is a
measurable subset of R,

00:59:56.940 --> 01:00:05.910 align:middle line:84%
we define L plus of E. This is
a set of all extended real-value

01:00:05.910 --> 01:00:09.240 align:middle line:84%
functions, non-negative
real-value functions that are

01:00:09.240 --> 01:00:09.960 align:middle line:90%
measurable .

01:00:09.960 --> 01:00:20.040 align:middle line:90%


01:00:20.040 --> 01:00:23.820 align:middle line:84%
And now what the
goal is is to define

01:00:23.820 --> 01:00:28.680 align:middle line:84%
the integral of a function
like that's in this class.

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:30.780 align:middle line:84%
And it may be an
infinite number.

01:00:30.780 --> 01:00:32.590 align:middle line:90%
It may not be.

01:00:32.590 --> 01:00:34.980 align:middle line:84%
And in order to do
that we're first

01:00:34.980 --> 01:00:38.190 align:middle line:84%
going to define how to
integrate the simplest

01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:39.640 align:middle line:90%
type of functions--

01:00:39.640 --> 01:00:40.860 align:middle line:90%
well, simple functions.

01:00:40.860 --> 01:00:45.480 align:middle line:90%


01:00:45.480 --> 01:00:51.395 align:middle line:90%
So let phi be a simple function.

01:00:51.395 --> 01:00:55.850 align:middle line:90%


01:00:55.850 --> 01:00:58.700 align:middle line:84%
And let's write phi
in this canonical way.

01:00:58.700 --> 01:01:01.830 align:middle line:90%


01:01:01.830 --> 01:01:15.610 align:middle line:84%
So phi is equal to
a sum of aj chi Aj

01:01:15.610 --> 01:01:26.480 align:middle line:90%
where for all i not equal to j--

01:01:26.480 --> 01:01:38.350 align:middle line:84%
well, first off, for all
j, Aj is a subset of E.

01:01:38.350 --> 01:01:45.143 align:middle line:84%
For all i not equal to j,
Ai intersect Aj is empty.

01:01:45.143 --> 01:01:46.060 align:middle line:90%
So these are disjoint.

01:01:46.060 --> 01:01:49.130 align:middle line:90%


01:01:49.130 --> 01:02:14.346 align:middle line:84%
And their union
gives me the set E.

01:02:14.346 --> 01:02:25.810 align:middle line:84%
The Lebesgue integral of
the simple function phi

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:29.980 align:middle line:84%
is the number, which I
said is the simplest way,

01:02:29.980 --> 01:02:33.350 align:middle line:84%
or what you would
expect to define--

01:02:33.350 --> 01:02:45.540 align:middle line:90%


01:02:45.540 --> 01:02:49.350 align:middle line:84%
how to define the integral
of a simple function.

01:02:49.350 --> 01:02:55.700 align:middle line:90%
So we know how to measure sets.

01:02:55.700 --> 01:03:00.440 align:middle line:84%
And remember we
initially built up

01:03:00.440 --> 01:03:06.620 align:middle line:84%
measure so that
the integral, which

01:03:06.620 --> 01:03:09.570 align:middle line:84%
would be a theory of
area underneath the curve

01:03:09.570 --> 01:03:10.070 align:middle line:90%
should be--

01:03:10.070 --> 01:03:13.430 align:middle line:90%


01:03:13.430 --> 01:03:15.620 align:middle line:84%
the integral of the
indicator function

01:03:15.620 --> 01:03:19.820 align:middle line:84%
should be the area
underneath the curve of 1,

01:03:19.820 --> 01:03:22.910 align:middle line:84%
area being the measure of
the set where that indicator

01:03:22.910 --> 01:03:26.070 align:middle line:90%
function is 1.

01:03:26.070 --> 01:03:32.370 align:middle line:84%
And therefore by
linearity, this will

01:03:32.370 --> 01:03:35.320 align:middle line:84%
be how we define the Lebesgue
integral of a simple function.

01:03:35.320 --> 01:03:43.470 align:middle line:84%
So the loop Lebesgue of phi is
the following number, E phi--

01:03:43.470 --> 01:03:45.240 align:middle line:90%
this is how it's defined--

01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:53.505 align:middle line:84%
sum from j equals 1
to n Aj measure of Aj.

01:03:53.505 --> 01:04:03.770 align:middle line:90%


01:04:03.770 --> 01:04:08.990 align:middle line:84%
And this number
could be infinite.

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:13.030 align:middle line:90%


01:04:13.030 --> 01:04:19.090 align:middle line:84%
All right, and instead of
writing just the integral

01:04:19.090 --> 01:04:21.670 align:middle line:84%
over E of phi, I might
add a dx in there.

01:04:21.670 --> 01:04:26.116 align:middle line:90%


01:04:26.116 --> 01:04:27.855 align:middle line:84%
I'm just warning
you ahead of time.

01:04:27.855 --> 01:04:36.984 align:middle line:90%


01:04:36.984 --> 01:04:38.980 align:middle line:84%
All right, so this is
the Lebesgue integral

01:04:38.980 --> 01:04:39.970 align:middle line:90%
of a simple function.

01:04:39.970 --> 01:04:42.250 align:middle line:84%
Again, we split it up
into this canonical way

01:04:42.250 --> 01:04:45.880 align:middle line:84%
where it's just the indicator
function of disjoint sets

01:04:45.880 --> 01:04:50.360 align:middle line:84%
which whose union gives
the set of the domain

01:04:50.360 --> 01:04:55.270 align:middle line:84%
and where these numbers out
in front, these coefficients,

01:04:55.270 --> 01:05:04.420 align:middle line:90%
give you the numbers that go--

01:05:04.420 --> 01:05:05.810 align:middle line:90%
of the elements of the range.

01:05:05.810 --> 01:05:10.510 align:middle line:84%
So, for example again,
I mean, this is--

01:05:10.510 --> 01:05:16.660 align:middle line:84%
let's say A is from-- or let's
say E is the interval a, b.

01:05:16.660 --> 01:05:18.880 align:middle line:90%
You can check that--

01:05:18.880 --> 01:05:22.070 align:middle line:90%


01:05:22.070 --> 01:05:24.790 align:middle line:84%
well, I mean, it's just
from the definition

01:05:24.790 --> 01:05:31.640 align:middle line:84%
that if my simple function is,
in fact, these sets are just

01:05:31.640 --> 01:05:43.650 align:middle line:84%
intervals, so if this is what
my simple function looks like,

01:05:43.650 --> 01:05:47.100 align:middle line:84%
it takes finitely many values,
this one, this one, this one,

01:05:47.100 --> 01:05:49.080 align:middle line:90%
this one, this one.

01:05:49.080 --> 01:05:51.990 align:middle line:84%
And on these sets
whose disjoint union

01:05:51.990 --> 01:06:02.030 align:middle line:84%
forms a, b, then as
I've defined and since--

01:06:02.030 --> 01:06:04.400 align:middle line:84%
as I've defined the integral
and since the measure

01:06:04.400 --> 01:06:08.450 align:middle line:84%
of an interval equals the
length of the interval,

01:06:08.450 --> 01:06:13.115 align:middle line:84%
again, you should count
this as some more comments.

01:06:13.115 --> 01:06:23.680 align:middle line:90%


01:06:23.680 --> 01:06:27.790 align:middle line:84%
This integral
equals the sum of--

01:06:27.790 --> 01:06:31.220 align:middle line:90%
so this would be aj.

01:06:31.220 --> 01:06:36.420 align:middle line:90%


01:06:36.420 --> 01:06:39.540 align:middle line:84%
Then how I've defined
the Lebesgue integral,

01:06:39.540 --> 01:06:49.720 align:middle line:84%
it spits out the area
underneath this simple function

01:06:49.720 --> 01:06:55.060 align:middle line:84%
that is taking these
values on these intervals.

01:06:55.060 --> 01:06:55.900 align:middle line:90%
I hope that's clear.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:58.233 align:middle line:84%
It's towards the end of the
day so maybe my explanations

01:06:58.233 --> 01:07:03.580 align:middle line:84%
are getting a bit wonky,
but I hope it's clear.

01:07:03.580 --> 01:07:13.490 align:middle line:90%


01:07:13.490 --> 01:07:16.130 align:middle line:84%
And then we're going to use this
definition of simple-- of how

01:07:16.130 --> 01:07:17.380 align:middle line:90%
to integrate simple functions.

01:07:17.380 --> 01:07:20.960 align:middle line:84%
We're going to extend it
to general elements of L

01:07:20.960 --> 01:07:23.600 align:middle line:84%
plus of E, the non-negative
measurable functions.

01:07:23.600 --> 01:07:29.400 align:middle line:84%
But first let's prove
a few properties

01:07:29.400 --> 01:07:32.550 align:middle line:84%
of how we've defined the
integral for simple functions.

01:07:32.550 --> 01:07:45.010 align:middle line:90%


01:07:45.010 --> 01:07:49.120 align:middle line:90%
Let's take two simple functions.

01:07:49.120 --> 01:07:54.270 align:middle line:84%
First is that if c is
bigger than or equal to 0,

01:07:54.270 --> 01:07:59.850 align:middle line:84%
then the integral of
c times phi over E,

01:07:59.850 --> 01:08:03.960 align:middle line:84%
this is equal to c times
the integral of E phi.

01:08:03.960 --> 01:08:08.530 align:middle line:90%


01:08:08.530 --> 01:08:20.540 align:middle line:84%
Two, the Lebesgue integral
over E of phi plus psi

01:08:20.540 --> 01:08:24.020 align:middle line:84%
is equal to the
Lebesgue integral

01:08:24.020 --> 01:08:30.660 align:middle line:84%
of phi plus the Lebesgue
integral of psi.

01:08:30.660 --> 01:08:41.439 align:middle line:84%
And third property is if phi is
less than or equal to psi on E,

01:08:41.439 --> 01:08:43.130 align:middle line:84%
so I'm writing that
shorthand by that.

01:08:43.130 --> 01:08:45.760 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm saying in that
statement is for all x in E,

01:08:45.760 --> 01:08:49.450 align:middle line:84%
phi of x is less than or
equal to psi of x, then

01:08:49.450 --> 01:08:52.960 align:middle line:84%
what you expect is
the integral of phi

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:55.344 align:middle line:84%
is less than or equal
to the integral of psi.

01:08:55.344 --> 01:09:07.609 align:middle line:90%


01:09:07.609 --> 01:09:12.725 align:middle line:84%
And let me include one
more very simple property.

01:09:12.725 --> 01:09:16.819 align:middle line:90%


01:09:16.819 --> 01:09:24.160 align:middle line:84%
If f is-- let's add
a little punctuation.

01:09:24.160 --> 01:09:31.899 align:middle line:84%
If f is a measurable
subset of E,

01:09:31.899 --> 01:09:35.430 align:middle line:84%
then phi which is a
simple function on E

01:09:35.430 --> 01:09:37.200 align:middle line:90%
is also a simple function on f.

01:09:37.200 --> 01:09:40.468 align:middle line:84%
It takes only finitely
many values on f as well,

01:09:40.468 --> 01:09:42.135 align:middle line:84%
and therefore it has
an integral over f.

01:09:42.135 --> 01:09:44.990 align:middle line:90%


01:09:44.990 --> 01:09:55.500 align:middle line:84%
And my claim is that is
equal to the integral over E

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:58.440 align:middle line:84%
of phi times the
indicator function of f.

01:09:58.440 --> 01:10:02.850 align:middle line:90%


01:10:02.850 --> 01:10:24.790 align:middle line:84%
And that's less than or
equal to the integral over E.

01:10:24.790 --> 01:10:27.662 align:middle line:84%
Now I will leave
problem 4 to you.

01:10:27.662 --> 01:10:29.120 align:middle line:84%
I might even put
on the assignment.

01:10:29.120 --> 01:10:34.730 align:middle line:84%
But it will follow once you've
seen how we prove 1, 2, and 3.

01:10:34.730 --> 01:10:37.170 align:middle line:84%
You'll be like, OK, so
I know how to do this.

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:45.640 align:middle line:84%
So number 1 is
pretty easy simply

01:10:45.640 --> 01:10:49.245 align:middle line:84%
because multiplying by
a non-negative constant

01:10:49.245 --> 01:10:51.370 align:middle line:84%
just carries through and
just changes the constant,

01:10:51.370 --> 01:10:53.680 align:middle line:90%
but not the sets.

01:10:53.680 --> 01:11:02.035 align:middle line:84%
So c times phi is equal
to c times a sub j.

01:11:02.035 --> 01:11:07.120 align:middle line:90%


01:11:07.120 --> 01:11:12.340 align:middle line:84%
And therefore the integral
of c times phi over E,

01:11:12.340 --> 01:11:14.980 align:middle line:84%
this is by definition
equal to j--

01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:19.440 align:middle line:84%
sum from j equals 1 to
n of c times a sub j

01:11:19.440 --> 01:11:22.830 align:middle line:90%
measure of a sub j.

01:11:22.830 --> 01:11:28.770 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to c times
sum from j equals 1 to n

01:11:28.770 --> 01:11:32.220 align:middle line:90%
of a sub j m of a sub j.

01:11:32.220 --> 01:11:46.670 align:middle line:84%
And this equals c times
the integral of phi over E.

01:11:46.670 --> 01:11:51.570 align:middle line:84%
All right, so to
prove 2 we write

01:11:51.570 --> 01:12:00.390 align:middle line:84%
phi in this canonical
form, sum over a sub j

01:12:00.390 --> 01:12:04.020 align:middle line:84%
times the indicator
function of a sub j's

01:12:04.020 --> 01:12:05.820 align:middle line:84%
where, again, these
sets are disjoint.

01:12:05.820 --> 01:12:08.850 align:middle line:84%
And their union gives
me E. And then also I

01:12:08.850 --> 01:12:14.430 align:middle line:84%
do the same thing for
phis, now k equals 1 to m,

01:12:14.430 --> 01:12:19.400 align:middle line:84%
maybe take on different
number of values,

01:12:19.400 --> 01:12:25.680 align:middle line:84%
bk, chi, bk, where again
the bk's is are disjoint

01:12:25.680 --> 01:12:50.440 align:middle line:90%
and their union gives me E.

01:12:50.440 --> 01:12:56.080 align:middle line:84%
So since the union
of the Aj's gives me

01:12:56.080 --> 01:13:09.570 align:middle line:84%
E and the union of
the bk's give me,

01:13:09.570 --> 01:13:16.860 align:middle line:84%
this implies that if I want to
look at one of these Aj's it's

01:13:16.860 --> 01:13:26.360 align:middle line:84%
equal to a union of a certain
type, is equal to the union k

01:13:26.360 --> 01:13:33.470 align:middle line:84%
equals 1 to m of Aj intersect
Bk because this is just

01:13:33.470 --> 01:13:36.230 align:middle line:84%
going to be equal
to Aj intersect

01:13:36.230 --> 01:13:41.850 align:middle line:84%
the union of the Bk's, but the
union of the Bk's gives me E,

01:13:41.850 --> 01:13:54.830 align:middle line:84%
and similarly for the Bk's
because the union of Aj's gives

01:13:54.830 --> 01:14:00.220 align:middle line:90%
me E.

01:14:00.220 --> 01:14:03.985 align:middle line:84%
And, again, and these
unions are disjoint.

01:14:03.985 --> 01:14:13.620 align:middle line:90%


01:14:13.620 --> 01:14:16.530 align:middle line:84%
The Aj's are disjoint
from each other.

01:14:16.530 --> 01:14:18.490 align:middle line:84%
The Bj's are disjoint
from each other.

01:14:18.490 --> 01:14:22.110 align:middle line:84%
So for all j and
k, Aj intersect Bk

01:14:22.110 --> 01:14:27.900 align:middle line:84%
is going to be disjoint from
another Aj prime intersect Bk

01:14:27.900 --> 01:14:31.170 align:middle line:84%
prime when j or k do
not equal each other.

01:14:31.170 --> 01:14:38.770 align:middle line:90%


01:14:38.770 --> 01:14:42.880 align:middle line:84%
So since these are
disjoint unions,

01:14:42.880 --> 01:15:02.090 align:middle line:84%
we have from the additivity
property of Lebesgue measure,

01:15:02.090 --> 01:15:06.370 align:middle line:84%
we get that the
integral over phi E

01:15:06.370 --> 01:15:10.630 align:middle line:84%
plus the integral
over E of psi, this

01:15:10.630 --> 01:15:15.520 align:middle line:84%
is equal to, by
definition, j equals 1 to n

01:15:15.520 --> 01:15:20.185 align:middle line:90%
of Aj measure of Aj plus--

01:15:20.185 --> 01:15:23.990 align:middle line:90%


01:15:23.990 --> 01:15:30.080 align:middle line:90%
equals 1 to m Bk measure of Bk.

01:15:30.080 --> 01:15:34.250 align:middle line:84%
Now, Aj is written as
this disjoint union.

01:15:34.250 --> 01:15:35.930 align:middle line:84%
And therefore the
measure of Aj is

01:15:35.930 --> 01:15:48.650 align:middle line:84%
equal to the sum of the measure
of these, of Aj intersect Bk

01:15:48.650 --> 01:15:49.640 align:middle line:90%
plus--

01:15:49.640 --> 01:15:51.020 align:middle line:90%
and then the same thing here.

01:15:51.020 --> 01:15:54.260 align:middle line:84%
The measure of-- so Bk is
equal to this disjoint union.

01:15:54.260 --> 01:15:57.860 align:middle line:84%
So its measure is the
sum of the measure.

01:15:57.860 --> 01:16:01.805 align:middle line:90%
So this sum now includes j.

01:16:01.805 --> 01:16:09.960 align:middle line:90%


01:16:09.960 --> 01:16:18.790 align:middle line:84%
So let me just rewrite this as
sum over jk, aj plus bk measure

01:16:18.790 --> 01:16:21.170 align:middle line:90%
of age aj bk.

01:16:21.170 --> 01:16:26.920 align:middle line:90%


01:16:26.920 --> 01:16:36.800 align:middle line:84%
But the point is that the sum
of these two simple functions

01:16:36.800 --> 01:16:41.690 align:middle line:84%
you can check, this is
equal to the sum over jk

01:16:41.690 --> 01:16:46.220 align:middle line:84%
of Aj plus Bk
times the indicator

01:16:46.220 --> 01:16:50.045 align:middle line:90%
function of Aj intersect Bk.

01:16:50.045 --> 01:16:53.620 align:middle line:90%


01:16:53.620 --> 01:16:58.030 align:middle line:84%
So at those x's
when phi equals Aj,

01:16:58.030 --> 01:17:03.670 align:middle line:84%
and at those x's where phi
equals Bk, then I'm in this set

01:17:03.670 --> 01:17:05.140 align:middle line:90%
and the two sides agree.

01:17:05.140 --> 01:17:10.350 align:middle line:90%


01:17:10.350 --> 01:17:18.050 align:middle line:84%
This implies that the integral
over E of phi plus psi

01:17:18.050 --> 01:17:30.330 align:middle line:84%
is equal to measure
of Aj intersect

01:17:30.330 --> 01:17:38.640 align:middle line:84%
Bk, which as we just
saw is equal to the sum

01:17:38.640 --> 01:17:39.570 align:middle line:90%
of the integrals.

01:17:39.570 --> 01:17:44.440 align:middle line:90%


01:17:44.440 --> 01:17:45.610 align:middle line:90%
So that was 2.

01:17:45.610 --> 01:17:50.270 align:middle line:90%
3 is not too difficult either.

01:17:50.270 --> 01:17:53.290 align:middle line:84%
So, again, let's
assume that phi and psi

01:17:53.290 --> 01:17:58.210 align:middle line:84%
are written in that way,
in this canonical way.

01:17:58.210 --> 01:18:08.350 align:middle line:84%
Then for all x in E, phi of x
is less than or equal to psi x.

01:18:08.350 --> 01:18:20.960 align:middle line:84%
This is equivalent to aj is less
than or equal to bk whenever

01:18:20.960 --> 01:18:25.040 align:middle line:90%
aj in bk is nonempty.

01:18:25.040 --> 01:18:33.760 align:middle line:90%


01:18:33.760 --> 01:18:35.950 align:middle line:84%
Thus, again, we're going
to use the additivity

01:18:35.950 --> 01:18:38.110 align:middle line:84%
of the Lebesgue
measure and the fact

01:18:38.110 --> 01:18:40.660 align:middle line:84%
that the unions of
these Bk's give me E,

01:18:40.660 --> 01:18:44.980 align:middle line:84%
then if I look at the
interval of E of phi,

01:18:44.980 --> 01:18:52.330 align:middle line:84%
this is sum j equals 1
to n aj measure of Aj.

01:18:52.330 --> 01:19:02.620 align:middle line:84%
This is equal to the sum jk
aj times the measure of Aj Bk

01:19:02.620 --> 01:19:06.220 align:middle line:84%
because the Aj is a union
over Aj intersect Bks.

01:19:06.220 --> 01:19:08.140 align:middle line:90%
And that's a disjoint union.

01:19:08.140 --> 01:19:13.880 align:middle line:84%
And now whenever
this is non-zero,

01:19:13.880 --> 01:19:21.620 align:middle line:84%
that means that Aj
intersect Bk is nonempty.

01:19:21.620 --> 01:19:24.230 align:middle line:84%
And therefore that aj appearing
there is going to be less than

01:19:24.230 --> 01:19:26.090 align:middle line:90%
or equal to Bk.

01:19:26.090 --> 01:19:28.460 align:middle line:84%
And whenever this is
0, well whatever's

01:19:28.460 --> 01:19:30.860 align:middle line:84%
there is almost always
less than or equal to Bk

01:19:30.860 --> 01:19:33.350 align:middle line:84%
times the measure
of Aj intersect Bk.

01:19:33.350 --> 01:19:37.850 align:middle line:84%
So this is less
than or equal to jk

01:19:37.850 --> 01:19:44.240 align:middle line:90%
bk measure of Aj intersect Bk.

01:19:44.240 --> 01:19:51.790 align:middle line:90%


01:19:51.790 --> 01:19:54.970 align:middle line:84%
So whenever this is-- so maybe
that previous explanation

01:19:54.970 --> 01:19:55.540 align:middle line:90%
was not good.

01:19:55.540 --> 01:20:00.730 align:middle line:84%
Whenever this is nonempty I
will always have aj less than

01:20:00.730 --> 01:20:01.510 align:middle line:90%
or equal to bk.

01:20:01.510 --> 01:20:02.560 align:middle line:84%
So I should have
just said that I

01:20:02.560 --> 01:20:04.893 align:middle line:84%
don't know what I was going
on about measure zero stuff,

01:20:04.893 --> 01:20:06.130 align:middle line:90%
but ignore that.

01:20:06.130 --> 01:20:09.850 align:middle line:84%
Whenever this is nonempty,
like I just said a minute ago,

01:20:09.850 --> 01:20:12.700 align:middle line:84%
aj is less than or
equal to bk, all right.

01:20:12.700 --> 01:20:19.360 align:middle line:90%


01:20:19.360 --> 01:20:21.100 align:middle line:90%
And now we just reverse course.

01:20:21.100 --> 01:20:29.710 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to k equals
1 to m of bk measure of Bk

01:20:29.710 --> 01:20:34.450 align:middle line:84%
because union over j's
of these sets give me Bk.

01:20:34.450 --> 01:20:39.190 align:middle line:84%
And this is equal to the
integral of psi of E.

01:20:39.190 --> 01:20:44.350 align:middle line:84%
And, again, I will leave 4 to
you as a very simple exercise.

01:20:44.350 --> 01:20:50.130 align:middle line:90%


01:20:50.130 --> 01:20:54.190 align:middle line:90%
OK, I'm about out of time.

01:20:54.190 --> 01:20:57.210 align:middle line:84%
So what we've done is
we've defined the Lebesgue

01:20:57.210 --> 01:20:58.950 align:middle line:90%
integral of a simple function.

01:20:58.950 --> 01:21:01.660 align:middle line:90%


01:21:01.660 --> 01:21:07.690 align:middle line:84%
And as that picture shows, I
hope, or at least convinces you

01:21:07.690 --> 01:21:12.340 align:middle line:84%
that if the simple function
takes on that of a step

01:21:12.340 --> 01:21:16.540 align:middle line:84%
function, meaning the
Aj's are just intervals,

01:21:16.540 --> 01:21:19.930 align:middle line:84%
then the Lebesgue integral
of that step function

01:21:19.930 --> 01:21:22.300 align:middle line:84%
will, in fact, be the
area underneath phi.

01:21:22.300 --> 01:21:26.610 align:middle line:90%
So it's, again, this is--

01:21:26.610 --> 01:21:28.860 align:middle line:84%
you can think of
that as two ways,

01:21:28.860 --> 01:21:33.000 align:middle line:84%
as the Lebesgue integral
giving a theory of the area

01:21:33.000 --> 01:21:35.460 align:middle line:90%
underneath the curve.

01:21:35.460 --> 01:21:42.160 align:middle line:84%
You may also think of that
as being the first indication

01:21:42.160 --> 01:21:44.740 align:middle line:84%
that when I have a
Riemann integral function,

01:21:44.740 --> 01:21:47.860 align:middle line:84%
it will be also Lebesgue
integral because if I have

01:21:47.860 --> 01:21:50.290 align:middle line:84%
a step function like
phi is in the picture,

01:21:50.290 --> 01:21:52.900 align:middle line:84%
then that is a Riemann
integral function.

01:21:52.900 --> 01:21:54.400 align:middle line:84%
And the Riemann
integral is the area

01:21:54.400 --> 01:21:59.290 align:middle line:84%
underneath the curve, which
also agrees with the definition

01:21:59.290 --> 01:22:01.040 align:middle line:90%
of the Lebesgue integral.

01:22:01.040 --> 01:22:05.840 align:middle line:84%
So that, like I
said, should maybe

01:22:05.840 --> 01:22:11.750 align:middle line:84%
indicate that the Lebesgue
integral reduces to the Riemann

01:22:11.750 --> 01:22:15.770 align:middle line:84%
integral whenever we're
integrating a Riemann

01:22:15.770 --> 01:22:18.630 align:middle line:90%
integrable function.

01:22:18.630 --> 01:22:23.870 align:middle line:84%
And so next time we
will define the integral

01:22:23.870 --> 01:22:30.980 align:middle line:84%
of a non-negative
measurable function using

01:22:30.980 --> 01:22:33.890 align:middle line:84%
how we've defined the
integral of simple functions,

01:22:33.890 --> 01:22:41.050 align:middle line:84%
and prove some basic
properties, including

01:22:41.050 --> 01:22:44.140 align:middle line:84%
two of the main
convergence theorems

01:22:44.140 --> 01:22:47.240 align:middle line:84%
that go along with this
theory of integration.

01:22:47.240 --> 01:22:49.230 align:middle line:90%
So we'll stop there.

01:22:49.230 --> 01:22:55.000 align:middle line:90%